  "rules": {
    "retail_object_and_rating_boundary_v1": "The PlayStation Classic page is a seller snapshot at 86.22 dollars with no posted review; the generic 620-game box is 27.99 dollars with a 40-percent-of-100 aggregate over twelve reviews and explicit AV-only, repeated-game, and lower-picture-quality disclosures; the RetroPie page is a microSD card at 59.99 dollars with a 65-percent-of-100 aggregate over twelve reviews and conflicting 7,749 versus 10,000-plus game language. They are not three matched console configurations, none shows thousands of reviews, and none independently establishes latency, controller feel, legal game provenance, long-term reliability, or the father's fit.",
    "latency_pipeline_and_emulation_scope_v1": "A console emulator makes a host behave like a guest console; incomplete emulators can produce defects, but this general mechanism does not quantify a current product. Display lag is the delay between a sent signal and a display beginning to show it and is distinct from pixel response time. Therefore a complaint called lag must be localized to exact emulator or game timing, output mode, display processing, controller path, and the recipient's tolerance under a repeatable test rather than attributed automatically to emulation.",
    "model_specific_mini_contrast_v1": "The PlayStation Classic evidence binds its two non-analog, non-vibration controllers, nine PAL games at 50 Hz instead of 60 Hz, possible slower response for NTSC-expectant players, and negative critical reception to that exact product. The NES Classic evidence instead binds a static thirty-game library, 60 Hz US releases and 720p output, well-received emulation with limited mapper support, and criticism of a short controller cord and minor glitches to that different exact product. This contrast defeats both universal claims: emulation is not uniformly bad, and a mini-console label does not guarantee quality.",
    "recipient_preference_transfer_limit_v1": "One author wants a simple preloaded handheld but suspects dropshipping or overpricing; another values short family sessions and a conventional controller shape; a third expresses PS1 nostalgia while replies name many different favorite systems. These are individual preference signals and question generators, not a population distribution or evidence of the father's game library, nostalgia target, controller feel, setup tolerance, or sensitivity to delay.",
    "matched_father_trial_v1": "First record exact product identity, region, included games and versions, complete hardware and controllers, video output and adapter needs, storage or host requirements, seller, condition, warranty, return window, and total cost. Ask the father privately for target games, era, session style, controller shape, multiplayer needs, setup tolerance, and display. Under a return window compare the same target game or closest matched timing-sensitive scene on the same display and game mode, with game mode enabled and the same connection path; record controller comfort and directional response, audiovisual behavior, startup friction, game-list fit, and repeatable noticed delay against predeclared thresholds. Defer if identity, game provenance, compatibility, returnability, or a realistic recipient trial is unavailable.",
    "evidence_bounded_retro_gift_decision_v1": "No universal mini-console winner or universal emulation rejection is supported. Buy only an exact, returnable configuration whose identity and contents are verified, whose games and controller match the father's expressed preferences, and whose same-display recipient trial clears predeclared latency, comfort, setup, and value thresholds. Otherwise choose a different matched legal route or defer; the three frozen listings alone do not justify a purchase."
